:: Ending C Skepticism [nobr ending skepticism] {"position":"6800,2800","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "endingCSkepticism">>
        <<speaker "You">>
        No.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        I knew. Not that you'd refuse specifically. But I knew there was a version of you that would get to this room and look at that panel and see not a choice but the last step in someone else's sequence. I built the sequence. I know what it looks like from inside it. You're right to refuse. Not tactically — tactically it costs you. But you're right. There's a difference between acting freely and acting at the end of a very long leash and feeling free. You found the difference. Most people don't. Go. The aliens won't stop you. Neither will I.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Leave the control room.|Death C]]
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>


:: Ending C Standard [nobr ending] {"position":"6700,2700","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "endingCStandard">>
        <<speaker "You">>
        No.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        A silence. The narrator does not argue.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        Alright. I won't pretend I'm not— I was hoping. I had a plan and the plan required this and now I am sitting here looking at the end of the plan and I realise I hadn't thought very far past it. Which is embarrassing, honestly. I'm usually better than that.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        He laughs, briefly and without enjoyment.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        But I want to say this. There are people on Earth who would not have survived this ship at all. There are people who would have broken in that awakening chamber and never left. You got out. You talked to things that have no business being talked to. You found the room. And then you said no. That is one of the most human things I have ever watched happen and I mean that without any irony. The refusal is the freedom. I'm stuck here either way. You at least got to choose. Go. The aliens won't stop you. Good luck down there. I mean it.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Leave the control room.|Death C]]
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>


:: Ending D [nobr missing-features ending] {"position":"6400,3250","size":"100,200"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "endingD">>
        You lunge at the narrator. The biochip disorientation makes the movement imprecise. The narrator does not flinch in the way a frightened person would. There is something resigned about it.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You worked it out. All of it. And this is still where you ended up. I don't know if that means the working out didn't matter or if it means it mattered more than anything.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        Of all the— I genuinely didn't predict this specific path. I've thought about it a great deal. Still didn't predict it.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        Yes. Alright. Yes. I knew this was possible. I told myself it wasn't likely. I was optimistic. That's embarrassing in retrospect.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        I have to admit. I was not entirely surprised.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Continue.|Ending D Continue]]
            [[Stop.|Ending D Stop]]
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>


:: Ending D Continue [nobr missing-features ending] {"position":"6700,3150","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "endingDContinue">>
        The narrator is incapacitated. You, still affected by the biochip malfunction, instinctively touch the panel to steady yourself. The ship activates.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You know what's interesting. I built a plan that required a person to do something willingly. And it turns out willingly has more interpretations than I accounted for. Congratulations. Sincerely. I mean that without irony, which I recognise is exactly what someone being ironic would say, so you'll have to take my word for it.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        The plan worked anyway. Through a path I didn't map. I have been lying here for approximately twenty seconds thinking about what that says about planning and I believe it is the most interesting thing that has happened on this ship.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You know what's interesting. The plan worked anyway. I genuinely had not predicted this specific path through it. I find it is a very rich topic. Congratulations. Sincerely.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Continue.|Death A]]
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>


:: Ending D Stop [nobr missing-features ending] {"position":"6700,3450","size":"100,100"}
<div class="textbox">
    <<ctp "endingDStop">>
        You release the narrator. A beat of stillness between you.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You stopped. After everything you knew about me. After all of it. You stopped. I think what it actually proves is something about you specifically. And I don't have a speech for that. You caught me without one. First time in a long time.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        You stopped. That's not nothing. You were angry enough to start and you still stopped. That gap — between what you wanted to do and what you did — that is the entire conversation I have been trying to have with these aliens for months and they cannot find it in any of their data. It lives in that pause. You just demonstrated it.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <<speaker "Narrator">>
        The panel is still there. I'm not going to ask you twice. But it's still there.
    <<ctpNext clear>>
        <div class="choice">
            [[Place hand on panel.|Ending A Compliance]]
            [[Leave the room.|Death C]] 
        </div> 
  <</ctp>>
</div>


